    effort: 'L',
    impact: 'High — K-Score\'s 6 factor weights and the R:R hard-reject floor are two of the most consequential numbers in the whole pipeline (K-Score gates every paper-trading entry\'s min_kscore check; the R:R floor hard-blocks entries outright) and both are permanently whatever a human picked once, with zero feedback loop checking whether they still reflect what actually predicts returns.',
    title: 'Several consequential thresholds are permanently hardcoded with no calibration loop at all — most notably K-Score\'s 6 factor weights and the regime-aware R:R hard-reject floor',
    what: 'Confirmed via code read: kscore.py\'s _WEIGHTS dict (technical/momentum/value/growth/volatility/relative_strength) has never been regressed against real forward returns — a factor with near-zero real predictive power could be weighted as heavily as a strong one forever. hard_rejects.py\'s min_rr_ratio (2.0) and regime_min_rr_ratio (3.0) are read only from cfg, which no self-tuning mechanism ever writes to. Also identified as never-calibrated: max_daily_loss_pct/max_consecutive_losses circuit breakers, sizer.py\'s confidence/research/consensus/earnings multiplier tables, and max_entry_gap_pct/max_signal_age_hours/stop_cooldown_hours.',
    fix: 'Add calibrate_kscore_weights() (ranking-engine) and calibrate_min_rr_ratio() (decision-engine or signal-engine) following the exact same pattern already proven out by calibrate_ta_weights/calibrate_conviction_weights: 70/30 chronological split, fit only on the train slice, apply only if validation-slice EV/win-rate beats the current value, record to TuneHistory. The other static thresholds (circuit breakers, sizer multiplier tables) are lower-priority — worth calibrating once the two above are proven out, not before.',
    implementedNote: 'First slice shipped 2026-07-16: calibrate_min_rr_ratio() only (kscore weight calibration deliberately deferred — a separate, larger ranking-engine change; this session scoped to the narrower, higher-confidence first step per the tracker\'s own recommendation). Landed in paper_trading_engine.py/paper_portfolio.py, NOT decision-engine — min_rr_ratio\'s real, live-consequential enforcement point is _should_enter() (paper_trading_engine.py), which every real paper-trading entry actually goes through; decision-engine\'s hard_rejects.py is a secondary copy used only by the /decide/{symbol} analysis endpoint. Followed calibrate_entry_weights()\'s own established pattern in the exact same file (not signal-engine\'s Redis-cached style-profile pattern, which doesn\'t fit this architecture): sweep candidate R:R floors [1.25...4.0] against real closed PaperTrade rows (rr_ratio_at_entry, pnl), 70/30 chronological split, pick the train-slice EV-maximizing threshold (subject to a 15-trade-per-candidate floor so a threshold surviving on a handful of lucky trades can\'t win), apply only if it beats the CURRENT live default\'s own validation-slice EV (not a fixed baseline — whatever is actually live right now, so repeated calibration runs compare against the truth, not a stale number). Key design decision: this REPLACES THE FALLBACK DEFAULT only — an explicit min_rr_ratio set in any portfolio\'s own config (settable via paper-portfolio.tsx\'s Settings UI) always wins, exactly as it already does today against the original hardcoded 2.0/3.0 literals; the calibrated value is consulted only via cfg.get("min_rr_ratio", _default_min_rr_ratio(...))\'s fallback argument, never overriding an explicit setting. Same file-cache + reload pattern as the existing _load_entry_weights()/reload_entry_weights() (min_rr_calibration.json on disk, in-process cache, force-reloaded after a new calibration). Every promoted calibration writes one TuneHistory row (parameter_class="entry_gate"). Wired into the same weekly scheduler block as calibrate_entry_weights (called directly, not via HTTP — same admin-user-dependency reasoning already documented for that job). New GET /paper-portfolio/min_rr_calibration + POST /paper-portfolio/calibrate-min-rr endpoints, plus a third card + button in paper-portfolio.tsx\'s ML Intelligence Status panel matching the existing RL/Entry-Calibration cards exactly, and a JOB_META entry in admin-health.tsx. 9 new tests (5 covering _default_min_rr_ratio()\'s file-cache/fallback/reload behavior directly against the real function; 4 covering the sweep logic\'s _ev_at() via source-text extraction, since paper_portfolio.py itself can\'t be cleanly imported under this repo\'s stubbed-sqlalchemy test setup) — both adversarially verified by reintroducing the exact bug (wrong regime-key lookup; inverted threshold comparison) and confirming the predicted failures before restoring. All 157 market-data tests pass; frontend typechecks clean.',
