    title: 'Set FRED_API_KEY + fix release-date vs reference-period modeling + replace the hardcoded _MACRO_2026 calendar with FRED\'s real release-date feed',
    what: 'Second, subtler gap found during design: sync_fred() stores event_date as the observation\'s REFERENCE PERIOD (obs["date"] — e.g. "2026-06-01" for June CPI data), not the RELEASE DATE (e.g. July 14, when BLS actually publishes June CPI). Meanwhile services/market-data/src/api/routes.py\'s hand-maintained `_MACRO_2026` list — the one actually shown on the frontend Events Calendar, and the one this session already found and fixed one wrong date in (July 2026 CPI, off by a day) — stores release dates but is fragile and won\'t auto-extend past 2026. These are two different axes wearing the same "event_date" name. Any "alert me when CPI drops" feature needs the release-date axis as its trigger schedule, which today only exists as a hand-maintained list with a known history of drifting from the real BLS/Fed calendar. Also: EconomicEvent.expected_value (shared/db/models.py:774) exists in the schema but nothing ever writes it — sync_fred() only ever sets actual_value, so there is no consensus/expected number stored anywhere despite the column being there.',
    fix: 'Set a free FRED_API_KEY in production. Add a second sync path using FRED\'s free `fred/releases/dates` endpoint (the official upcoming-release calendar per release, e.g. release ID for CPI) to populate a genuine release-date row per event — either a new column or a clearly-named second event_type family, distinct from the existing reference-period rows. Replace `_MACRO_2026` as the source of truth for the frontend Events Calendar with this real feed once it\'s live. Populate expected_value from a free proxy where available (e.g. the Cleveland Fed inflation nowcast for CPI/PCE) — label it "nowcast/prior" in the UI, never "consensus," since real Street consensus (Econoday etc.) is a paid data product this app does not have.',
    implementedNote: 'Partially built 2026-07-14/15 — the release-date calendar itself is live in production; expected_value nowcast is not yet done. FRED_API_KEY set in production .env (verified via a real POST /events/sync/economic call: fred_series=131, no longer "skipped: no_api_key"). Added _FRED_RELEASES (release_id → event_type/title/importance for 10 releases, verified against the live fred/series/release endpoint) and sync_fred_release_dates() in economic.py — a second, distinct sync path from sync_fred() using FRED\'s fred/release/dates endpoint (include_release_dates_with_no_data=true to see future dates), writing genuine {event_type}_release rows so they never collide with sync_fred()\'s existing reference-period rows under uq_economic_event. Wired into event-intelligence\'s scheduler (daily 06:15 UTC cron + an immediate run at startup). market-data\'s events_calendar() now prefers these real DB rows via new _macro_events_from_db(), falling back to the hand-maintained _MACRO_2026 list only for (type, date-range) combinations the DB has no row for yet — replacing _MACRO_2026 as the source of truth without deleting it as a safety net during rollout. Verified live in production: GET /stocks/events/calendar now returns cpi type="cpi" date="2026-08-12" sourced from the real FRED release calendar (description: "CPI Release (FRED release calendar)"), not the hardcoded list. 10 new tests across both services (test_fred_release_dates.py, test_macro_events_from_db.py), all adversarially verified by reintroducing the exact bugs (wrong conflict clause — DO UPDATE instead of DO NOTHING; wrong event_type reverse-mapping) and confirming the predicted failure before restoring. Remaining: EconomicEvent.expected_value nowcast (Cleveland Fed proxy) not started.',
