    title: 'Broker order history: EtradeBroker.list_orders() + GET /broker/connections/{id}/orders + Settings UI table',
    what: 'BrokerInterface already had an optional list_orders() method (defaults to NotImplementedError, same pattern as other broker-specific-only capabilities) but no broker implemented it and no API route or UI exposed it. E*Trade\'s own REST API already has a real, general (non-orderId-filtered) GET /v1/accounts/{key}/orders.json endpoint — the same one get_order() already calls with an orderId filter — so this was almost entirely wiring, not new integration surface.',
    fix: 'Implemented EtradeBroker.list_orders(account_id=None, status="open") calling orders.json without the orderId filter, with an internal-vocabulary-to-E*Trade-param status map (open/filled/cancelled/rejected -> OPEN/EXECUTED/CANCELLED/REJECTED; "all" omits the param entirely rather than passing something E*Trade doesn\'t recognize, which would silently return zero rows). Parses OrdersResponse.Order[] into BrokerOrder instances, converting E*Trade\'s epoch-millisecond placedTime into ISO8601 (wrapped in try/except so a malformed/missing timestamp degrades to None rather than crashing the whole call). Added a new optional placed_at field to the BrokerOrder dataclass (backward-compatible — every other broker leaves it None). New GET /broker/connections/{id}/orders route in broker.py: verifies the connection is authorized, calls list_orders(), catches NotImplementedError -> HTTP 501 (distinct from a real failure) and any other exception -> HTTP 502, returns a flat {"orders": [...]} array. Settings.tsx: new "Order History" button next to "Load Balance" per broker connection, rendering a table (Symbol/Side/Qty/Status/Filled Price/Placed) on success, a specific "not supported by this broker" message on a 501, and an empty-state message when the account genuinely has zero orders — three distinct states rather than collapsing "unsupported" and "empty" into the same blank UI.',
    implementedNote: 'Done 2026-07-17, same session as T257-ETRADE-PROD-SYSTEMATIC above. Added api.brokerOrderHistory() + the BrokerOrderHistoryItem type to api.ts, and brokerOrders state + handleLoadOrderHistory() to settings.tsx, distinguishing the 501-unsupported case (checks err.message.includes(\'501\')) from a genuine fetch failure so the UI can show "this broker doesn\'t support order history" instead of a generic error banner. 9 new tests (test_broker_order_history.py), all against the real EtradeBroker class with requests.get mocked (EtradeBroker only depends on requests/requests_oauthlib, both real installed packages, not part of this repo\'s conftest.py stub list, so it imports and runs normally under pytest — no source-text-extraction workaround needed here): multi-order parsing, status-vocabulary translation, epoch-ms-to-ISO8601 conversion, graceful None on a missing placedTime, status="open" correctly mapping to the "OPEN" query param, status="all" omitting the param entirely, an HTTP failure raising RuntimeError, an empty OrdersResponse returning [] (not None), and ManualBroker correctly inheriting the base interface\'s NotImplementedError rather than silently returning empty (which would look identical to "authorized but genuinely zero orders" to a caller). Adversarially verified the status-mapping test by temporarily sabotaging the param assignment to pass the internal vocabulary straight through unmapped (params["status"] = status), confirmed the dedicated test failed with \'open\' == \'OPEN\', then reverted. requests_oauthlib needed a local pip install to run these tests (already a real pinned dependency in requirements.txt; just missing from this local dev environment, not a stubbed dependency). Full market-data suite green after this addition; frontend typecheck clean.',
