    what: 'User reported "why all paper trading not trading now on prod." Root cause traced to _monitor_positions()\'s WAIT-decay branch: `last_non_wait_ts < now - timedelta(days=wait_days)`, where last_non_wait_ts comes from Signal.ts (a plain DateTime column, no timezone=True — always naive) and `now` is datetime.now(timezone.utc) (tz-aware). Python raises TypeError on any naive-vs-aware comparison, never silently coerces. Traced via git history: `now`\'s construction changed from datetime.utcnow() (naive) to datetime.now(timezone.utc) (aware) in commit c59a291 (2026-07-22, BUG233-BACKTESTWALLCLOCK, to support an injectable as_of parameter for backtesting) — this one comparison, ~250 lines later in the same function, was never updated to match. A DIFFERENT site in the same function (T195 signal-staleness gate, line ~4427) already correctly guards against this exact class (`sig.ts.replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc) if sig.ts.tzinfo is None else sig.ts`) — proving the bug class was known and handled everywhere else, just missed here. The blast radius is much larger than one trade: paper_trading_step()\'s ENTIRE per-portfolio loop (every portfolio\'s monitor + scan) is wrapped in ONE outer try/except — the moment ANY portfolio\'s _monitor_positions() call raises (triggered by that portfolio holding so much as one open trade currently on a WAIT signal), the exception propagates out of the whole function, aborting _scan_for_entries() for every OTHER portfolio in that same cycle too.',
    fix: 'Strip tzinfo from `now` at this one comparison site (`now.replace(tzinfo=None)`) rather than changing `now`\'s type globally — `now` is correctly aware elsewhere in the same function (e.g. `trade.exit_time = now`, though PaperTrade.exit_time is also a naive DateTime column so this doesn\'t currently matter either way). Audited every other `now`-involving comparison in the function and confirmed no other site has this defect — all others are either SQL-side `.where()` clauses (which Postgres/SQLAlchemy handle correctly regardless of Python-side tzinfo) or already correctly guarded (the T195 site above).',
