    impact: 'Critical — was firing on infrastructure failure rather than a market event, emailing every affected user a false "your setup has faded" alert, and because revert is ONE-SHOT the user had to manually re-add the watch.',
    what: 'Three links formed the chain, all confirmed by direct code read: (1) check_gamma_unwind_alerts() had `if not candidates: ... return` BEFORE the setex write of stockai:bearish_puts_watch — so a no-candidate cycle never refreshed the cache and it simply expired on its 6h TTL. (2) check_squeeze_watch_reverts() built _bearish_by_symbol from `_json.loads(_rc.get(...) or "[]")` inside a try/except that set {} on failure — so a missing, expired, or unparseable key yielded an empty dict INDISTINGUISHABLE from "the scan ran and genuinely found nothing". (3) The revert branch did `if bp is None or ...: metric_faded = True` — absence alone was proof of fade.',
    fix: 'Moved the cache write in check_gamma_unwind_alerts() to happen BEFORE the zero-candidate early return, so a genuinely-completed empty scan writes a real, fresh, empty cache (real information) rather than skipping the write entirely. In check_squeeze_watch_reverts(), added an explicit _bearish_cache_fresh flag (true only when the Redis GET returned real, parseable data) — the fade-detection branch now distinguishes "symbol present in a FRESH scan but no longer puts-dominant" (real fade) and "symbol absent from a FRESH scan" (real fade, since the scan genuinely completed and found nothing) from "cache missing/stale/unparseable" (metric_faded stays False — absence of data can no longer be read as proof of fade; only price_recovered, checked independently, can revert the watch on a stale-cache cycle).',
