    what: 'sync_fred_release_dates() writes 10 release event_types (cpi, ppi, gdp, nfp, pce, fed_funds, retail_sales, consumer_conf, housing_starts, jobless_claims). check_release_day_fast_poll() — verified to be the ONLY writer of actual_value to *_release rows anywhere in the codebase — filters `EconomicEvent.event_type.in_(list(_RELEASE_TO_FRED_SERIES.keys()))`, and that dict has only 5 keys, one of which (pce_release) maps to None and is skipped. So 6 of 10 types are never even fetched from the DB. fed_funds_release alone accounts for 113 past rows with zero actuals. SECOND root cause affecting even the 4 "pollable" types: the poll fetches the REFERENCE-PERIOD series (CPIAUCSL etc.) and writes obs[0].value — the newest reference observation, which sync_fred() already wrote to the plain `cpi` row. So the release row duplicates the reference row rather than capturing "what was just published", and if FRED has not yet attached the new observation during the poll window, obs[0] is LAST MONTH\'s value, silently written as this release\'s actual.',
