    implementedNote: 'FIXED 2026-08-06. Root cause 1: added all 5 missing entries to _RELEASE_TO_FRED_SERIES (retail_sales_release->RSXFS, consumer_conf_release->UMCSENT, housing_starts_release->HOUST, jobless_claims_release->ICSA, fed_funds_release->FEDFUNDS) — each pulled directly from economic.py\'s own _FRED_SERIES list (the reference-period series sync_fred() already polls), not guessed. pce_release previously mapped to an explicit None because no PCE series existed in _FRED_SERIES at all — added a real PCEPI (PCE Price Index) entry to _FRED_SERIES and pointed pce_release at it, so all 10 _FRED_RELEASES event_types now resolve to a real series_id, none to None. Root cause 2: added a realtime_start guard immediately before the value is extracted/written — obs[0] is just the newest reference-period observation on the series, with nothing previously confirming it was actually published TODAY rather than an earlier date sync_fred() had already captured; a genuine same-day release has realtime_start == today (verified live in this module\'s own pre-existing docstring), so anything else now `continue`s and waits for a later poll cycle within the same armed 8:30-9:59am ET window (re-tried every 2 minutes) rather than silently writing a stale value.\\n\\nDeliberately NOT expanded in scope: fed_funds_release\'s own 8:30-9:59am ET armed window may not actually match FEDFUNDS\' real monthly-average publication schedule (a genuine rate DECISION is already separately covered by check_fomc_statement_poll\'s 2pm ET RSS poll) — this is a real, narrower, separate gap from the one this item\'s own fix field asked to close (adding the missing series mappings + fixing observation selection), left undone rather than silently expanding scope into a cron-schedule redesign.\\n\\nTests: 5 new cases in test_macro_reaction.py (26-test file total, up from 21) — confirms all 10 _FRED_RELEASES event_types resolve to a non-None series_id (the exact regression this fix closes), confirms every mapped series_id is a real entry in _FRED_SERIES (guards against a typo\'d/fabricated series_id that would 404 silently forever), confirms pce_release specifically resolves to PCEPI not None, and 3 source-text checks for the realtime_start guard (present, gates BEFORE actual_value extraction/write, and `continue`s rather than raising/returning so one series lacking a fresh observation doesn\'t abort checking the other due-today releases in the same poll cycle). Removed the now-stale test_pce_release_is_explicitly_none_not_silently_missing (asserted the old, now-fixed None mapping).\\n\\nAdversarially verified: (1) reverted _RELEASE_TO_FRED_SERIES back to its original 5-entry form — 3 dedicated tests failed correctly; (2) removed the realtime_start guard entirely — the 3 dedicated guard tests failed correctly with a real ValueError (substring not found), confirming they test the actual guard\'s presence, not just its logical effect. Both reverted and confirmed passing again before finalizing. Full 198-test event-intelligence suite green; pyflakes clean (5 pre-existing warnings across both touched files confirmed via git stash to predate this change, unchanged).\n\nUPDATE 2026-08-07 — one-time backfill for already-stored rows, found during a post-batch data-correctness sweep the user explicitly requested: the fix above only prevents FUTURE staleness — check_release_day_fast_poll() only ever queries for releases due TODAY, so it can never revisit an old date. Confirmed live in production: 203 PAST-dated *_release rows across ALL 10 types (not just the 113 fed_funds_release the original finding cited) were still missing actual_value even after this fix deployed, since they predate the fix and the poll structurally cannot reach backward. Unlike the AUD264 earnings fiscal-quarter backfill (where the real announcement date was genuinely unrecoverable from the data source), FRED\'s own vintage query (realtime_start/realtime_end pinned to the SAME past date) can recover the exact historical value — confirmed live: FRED returns a different value for FEDFUNDS queried at a January vintage vs. "latest". New backfill_release_actual_values() (POST /events/economic/backfill_release_actuals) fills only actual_value/previous_value for past-dated rows — deliberately does NOT also generate an LLM reaction, since generate_reaction() reads the CURRENT market regime, which would frame a months-old release through today\'s market context rather than what was true at the time (a real design decision, not an oversight — matches this codebase\'s standing "no fabricated context is better than a wrong one" discipline). 10 new tests (test_macro_reaction_backfill.py) using the same real-in-memory-SQLite-plus-real-EconomicEvent-model technique already established for the earnings backfill — covers the fill-path, the no-reaction guarantee, future-dated rows being correctly ignored (not a bug — legitimately still pending), already-filled rows never being overwritten, the exact realtime_start/realtime_end query shape, missing-value/non-200 FRED responses degrading to skipped-not-filled, one symbol\'s failure not aborting the batch, and the no-API-key skip. A real per-test-isolation bug caught and fixed while writing these: EconomicEvent has a genuine UNIQUE(event_type, country, event_date) constraint, and this file\'s tests share one module-level in-memory engine — an autouse fixture clearing the table before every test was needed to avoid cross-test collisions on the same (type, date) pair. Adversarially verified 2 sabotage cycles against the real production code: adding a reaction_text write to the backfill path was caught by 2 tests; removing the past-date filter (which would have queried and attempted to fill legitimately-pending future rows) was caught by 1 test. Full 232-test event-intelligence suite green (up from 222); pyflakes clean (confirmed via git stash that both pre-existing warnings predate this change).',
