    what: '_parse_atom uses the Atom <updated> timestamp AS reading_date. multpl\'s shiller-pe is a MONTHLY series but the feed\'s <updated> refreshes daily. stale = age_days > 45 is computed off MAX(reading_date). So if multpl freezes the CAPE value but keeps refreshing <updated>, the daily sync inserts a new row stamped today carrying a stale value, age_days stays ~0 forever, `stale` never fires, and dq_check:cape_reading stays green. The 45-day threshold is unreachable by construction.',
    fix: 'Derive reading_date from the observation period the value actually belongs to, not the feed\'s publication timestamp; or add a separate value-unchanged-for-N-days check.',
    implementedNote: 'Built 2026-08-10. Re-verified the original framing directly against the live feed before fixing anything, and it needed correcting: multpl.com\'s Atom feed genuinely IS a live "current value" reading, not a stale monthly-cadence publication timestamp — its own <content> block literally says "Current" with a real intraday timestamp ("4:00 PM EDT, Mon Aug 10"), and the value really does fluctuate daily with the market (confirmed against real production rows: 42.39/42.12/42.19/42.28/41.51/... across consecutive days). So reading_date correctly advances every day the sync succeeds — the bug isn\'t that reading_date is wrong, it\'s that a healthy, correctly-advancing reading_date makes age_days > 45 structurally unable to ever fire, leaving no detector at all for the real failure mode: multpl serving a genuinely FROZEN number wrapped in a fresh-looking daily timestamp. Implemented the tracker\'s own second recommendation exactly: added a new, independent frozen-value check — counts how many of the most recent readings (capped at the same 45-day window) share the identical cape_value; stale now fires on EITHER age_days > 45 (a truly dead feed/job) OR the value being frozen for the full 45-day window despite fresh rows landing every day. New `frozen_value_days` field surfaced in the API response and on the intelligence.tsx card, which now distinguishes the two failure modes in its own warning copy ("Data may be stale (Nd old)" vs. "Value unchanged for Nd — source may be frozen") rather than one generic message. 7 new tests in test_cape_frozen_value_detection.py, run against the REAL get_latest_cape() function (SessionLocal monkeypatched to a fake session/rows, not a source-text extraction) — covering the frozen-day counter\'s exact boundary, the core regression case (age_days=0 with a value frozen for the full threshold must still flag stale=True), a direct confirmation that real fluctuating production data never false-positives, and a dedicated check that the ORIGINAL age-based detector still independently fires for a genuinely dead feed. Adversarially verified 2 guards by sabotage (disabling the frozen-value counter, removing it from the stale OR-condition), both caught and reverted. Also re-confirmed the tracker\'s own prior negative finding (39 cape_readings rows is expected, not thin) still holds — untouched by this fix. Zero regressions: event-intelligence 234->239 passed (+7 exactly, verified via git-stash baseline comparison, plus 2 pre-existing unrelated unused-import pyflakes warnings confirmed to predate this change).',
