    effort: 'M',
    impact: 'High — can hold a position through an entire decline (stop never fires), or trigger a phantom exit at a price that never traded, with exit_price recorded as if real.',
    what: 'BUG-MONITORPOS-STALEPRICE (2026-07-21) added a Redis consecutive-stale-cycle counter that escalates log.warning to log.error at 5 cycles. Confirmed: it GATED NOTHING. The code comment said so explicitly ("Never changes which price is actually used for exit math"). After the fallback, execution proceeded unconditionally with live_price = trade.current_price or trade.entry_price, and every stop/target comparison ran against it. Worse, each cycle re-wrote trade.current_price = live_price, so the stale value was laundered into a field that then LOOKED freshly updated. _best_price() shares the same chain, so _compute_equity and the sector-cap monitor also consume the frozen value.',
    fix: 'Make the escalation actually gate exit execution: past the threshold, skip stop/target evaluation for that trade (hold, do not exit on a price known to be stale) and surface it. Distinguish "no fresh quote" from "fresh quote at this price" rather than silently substituting.',
    implementedNote: 'FIXED 2026-08-07. Scoped precisely to the tracker\'s own fix description: _best_price()/_compute_equity() deliberately LEFT UNCHANGED — falling back to the cached price for PORTFOLIO VALUE (there\'s no better number to compute equity with when the feed is blind) is a genuinely different, correct concern from letting a STOP/TARGET COMPARISON act on a price that may no longer reflect reality; conflating the two would have been a scope-creeping overreach beyond what this item actually asked for.\\n\\nImplementation: a new `_price_is_stale_escalated` flag (initialized False at the very top of each trade\'s iteration, so a fresh-quote trade never inherits a prior trade\'s stale state) is set True the moment the existing escalation threshold fires. A new `elif _price_is_stale_escalated:` branch was inserted into the exit-evaluation if/elif chain, positioned as the SECOND branch — directly after the pre-existing BUG-PAPERPOS-DELISTED-FROZEN delisted check, and before every price-based branch (stop_hit/breakeven_stop/trailing_stop/target_reached/signal_exit/time_stop/hold_stall_timeout/momentum_fade/WAIT-decay). Because every one of those is itself an `elif`, Python\'s own chain semantics mean matching this ONE new branch correctly short-circuits ALL of them for that trade this cycle — no need to individually add a guard to each of the ~9 existing branches, which would have been far more invasive and error-prone. The new branch sets no exit_reason (a deliberate no-op — "Execute exit" below only fires `if exit_reason:`), so the position is simply held: no phantom exit at a fake price, and no stop silently failing to fire against a frozen number either, since nothing is evaluated against it at all this cycle. The delisted check itself is deliberately NOT gated by this new flag — a confirmed delisted stock has no real market left at all (a fundamentally different situation from a temporarily-blind feed), so it must still close out even if both conditions happen to co-occur for the same trade.\\n\\nTests: 6 new cases added to test_monitor_positions_stale_price.py (13 total, up from 7) — matching this file\'s own established source-text-extraction convention (the full function is 200+ lines with heavy Signal/RSI/regime dependencies disproportionate to this fix\'s scope). Covers: the escalation flag is actually set inside the threshold branch, it defaults False before the stale-check runs (and is initialized close enough to the top of the loop iteration that no other logic could run in between), the new elif is correctly positioned between the delisted check and the first price-based branch, the escalated branch itself never assigns exit_reason, and the delisted check\'s own code body (not the surrounding prose/comments, which legitimately name the flag for documentation) never references the new flag at all. One pre-existing test\'s docstring (test_staleness_tracking_never_changes_which_price_is_used_for_exit_math) was updated to stop claiming this fix is "diagnostic-only by design," since it no longer is — the assertion itself was unchanged and still correct (the fallback-price computation itself is untouched; only what happens LATER, in the exit chain, changed).\\n\\nAdversarially verified 2 sabotage cycles, both caught correctly: removing the `_price_is_stale_escalated = True` assignment (reverting to the original log-only bug) was caught by the dedicated flag-is-set test; removing the new `elif _price_is_stale_escalated:` branch entirely (reverting to the fully ungated chain) was caught by 2 dedicated tests, both via a real `ValueError: substring not found` — confirming they test the branch\'s actual presence, not just a coincidental string match. Both reverted and confirmed passing again before finalizing. Full 981-test market-data suite green after every revert (up from 976); pyflakes clean (3 pre-existing, unrelated warnings confirmed via git stash to predate this change, only a line number shifted).',
