    what: 'json.dumps() serializes float(\'nan\')/float(\'inf\')/float(\'-inf\') into the bare, non-standard tokens NaN/Infinity/-Infinity by default (allow_nan=True) — valid to Python\'s OWN json.loads() on the round trip, but NOT valid JSON, and Postgres\'s CAST(:rsns AS jsonb) is strict and rejects them with a real psycopg2.errors.InvalidTextRepresentation. Root cause traced live against real production data (not guessed): signals.py\'s own T233-SIG-RSI1 comment already documents that a thin-history stock (below the 26-bar slow-EMA warmup window) correctly gets a genuine NaN macd_hist from the canonical MACD function — every SCORING consumer of that value already treats a NaN comparison as False safely via Python semantics, but reasons["macd_hist"] = macd_hist persisted the raw NaN straight into the dict that both _bulk_persist() and signal_for() later json.dumps() before the jsonb cast. Confirmed live end-to-end on the EC2 signal-engine container: reproduced generate_all_signals("6951.HK") returning macd_hist=nan on all 4 horizons, and confirmed CAST(\\\'{"macd_hist": NaN}\\\' AS jsonb) fails against the real production Postgres with the exact same error text as the traceback.',
    fix: 'New _json_safe() in signals_shared.py — recursively replaces any non-finite float (nan/inf/-inf) with None, applied to reasons at both real INSERT call sites in routes.py before json.dumps(). Fixed at the JSON-serialization boundary generically (not by special-casing macd_hist) so any OTHER field that could carry a stray NaN/Inf in the future is covered too, matching this session\'s own updown_vol_ratio fix for the identical bug class.',
    implementedNote: 'FIXED 2026-08-12. Live-verified against the exact real production data that produced the failing rows, both before and after: pre-fix, generate_all_signals("6951.HK")\'s macd_hist=nan reproduced the real InvalidTextRepresentation error when cast against production Postgres directly; post-fix, _json_safe() converts it to null and the resulting JSON casts cleanly. New services/signal-engine/tests/test_json_safe_reasons.py (11 cases) — _json_safe() tested directly (nan/inf/-inf -> None, finite floats/non-floats pass through unchanged, recurses into nested dicts/lists, does not mutate the caller\'s dict, and a reproduction of the exact real production reasons shape round-trips to valid JSON with no bare NaN/Infinity token), plus 3 source-text regression checks on routes.py confirming both real call sites (_bulk_persist and signal_for — the latter is the LAST function in the file, so its body slices to end-of-file rather than a trailing "\\ndef ") use _json_safe() before json.dumps(), and a guard that no THIRD unguarded json.dumps(ai.reasons)/json.dumps(sig.reasons) call site exists anywhere in the file. Adversarially verified twice: reverted both call sites back to a bare json.dumps(ai.reasons) and confirmed exactly the 3 wiring-guard tests failed; separately reverted _json_safe()\'s own isfinite check and confirmed the 5 dedicated behavioral tests failed while the wiring tests (which only check routes.py\'s source, not the implementation) correctly stayed green — both sabotage cycles reverted and reconfirmed green. Full in-scope signal-engine suite green (314 passed, excluding the 4 pre-existing test_analyst_momentum.py failures already documented elsewhere in this file as predating this session); pyflakes clean (confirmed via git stash, zero new warnings).\n\nUPDATE 2026-08-12b — found and fixed a SECOND, independent half of this bug the SAME live-verification pass, right after the first fix deployed. Live-triggering the exact fixed call site (GET /signals/6951.HK?live=true&persist=true, the manual-refresh path) to confirm the DB write now succeeds instead surfaced a real 500: ValueError("Out of range float values are not JSON compliant: nan") — from Starlette\'s response serializer, not the DB layer. Root cause: the DB-write fix\'s _json_safe() call only sanitized a SEPARATE json.dumps() call for the SQL parameter — it deliberately does NOT mutate ai.reasons in place (see test_original_dict_is_not_mutated), so the raw NaN survived untouched all the way to signal_for()\'s own two response constructions (return {"symbol": ..., **asdict(ai)} for the ?style=X branch, and the equivalent all-styles branch), which Starlette\'s default JSONResponse serializes with STRICTER-than-json.dumps() defaults that reject NaN outright rather than emitting the non-standard token. Grepped every other asdict()/reasons usage in routes.py/calibration.py/outcomes.py first to confirm these were the only 2 unprotected response-construction sites (all other reasons reads are either mutations on an already-in-scope ai.reasons, DB-persisted reads that can only ever be NaN-free since a NaN row could never have written successfully, or plain scalar counts with no reasons involved at all). Fix: wrapped both return statements in _json_safe() directly (return _json_safe({...})) — the same proven function, applied at the response boundary this time instead of the DB-write boundary. 3 new tests added to the same test_json_safe_reasons.py (14 total): 2 source-text regression checks confirming both response constructions are wrapped, plus an integration-style test building the EXACT asdict()-plus-spread dict shape the real code produces (generators.signals can\'t be imported directly in this test environment, so a plain dict stands in for asdict(ai)) and confirming it composes with _json_safe() into genuinely valid JSON, not just that _json_safe() works on a reasons dict in isolation. Adversarially verified: reverted both return statements back to their unwrapped form and confirmed exactly the 2 dedicated wiring tests failed, reverted back, confirmed all 14 green again. Full in-scope signal-engine suite green (317 passed, up from 314, same 4 pre-existing unrelated failures); pyflakes clean. Re-deployed and live-verified a THIRD time: the same GET /signals/6951.HK?live=true&persist=true call that originally produced the 500 now returns a clean 200 against the live production container.',
