    impact: 'High — the whole point of T249-MARKETMOVER-P2\'s fast-reaction feature is same-day detection of a real macro release; a window that can close before the release actually posts silently defeats that promise with no error anywhere, and the gap is invisible until a user notices they never got the email.',
    what: 'User asked directly why they hadn\'t received a CPI reaction email on 2026-08-12 (a real, scheduled CPI release date). Live-traced: FRED\'s own realtime_start for that day\'s CPI observation confirmed the number WAS genuinely published on 2026-08-12 — but the poll\'s last scheduled check that morning (9:58am ET, the tail of its hour="8-9" / 8:30-9:59am ET cron window) still returned `found: 0`, meaning FRED had not yet attached the observation by then. The poll\'s own window then closed for the day (next run: tomorrow 8:00am ET), leaving the release permanently undetected until manually found and backfilled. The original window\'s own code comment claimed "8:30-10:00 ET... with margin for FRED\'s own 15-60min typical ingestion lag" — that assumption did not hold on this occasion.',
    fix: 'Widened the cron window from hour="8-9" (8:30-9:59am ET) to hour="8-12" (through 12:59pm ET) — still a cheap no-op on non-release days (the poll\'s own due_today DB query gates every FRED call before it ever fires), so the added cost is a handful of extra FRED calls, only on real release mornings, only for as long as a release genuinely remains undetected that day. Updated the function\'s own docstring to match.',
    implementedNote: 'FIXED 2026-08-12 (same day, live). Immediate manual recovery performed first: confirmed the real FRED value directly (CPIAUCSL, July 2026 = 332.813, realtime_start=2026-08-12), manually wrote it to the due economic_events row, and called generate_reaction() directly (the same function the poll itself would have called) to produce a real LLM reaction — verified check_macro_reaction_alerts() (the separate, unaffected 1-minute delivery job) picked it up and sent the email within its next cycle (confirmed via an explicit signal_alert.macro_reaction_sent log line for the real recipient). This recovery reused only existing, already-tested code paths — no new code was needed to unblock the user immediately.\n\nCode fix: widened services/event-intelligence/src/scheduler.py\'s check_release_day_fast_poll cron trigger from hour="8-9" to hour="8-12", and corrected job_check_release_day_fast_poll()\'s own docstring (previously said "8:30-10:00 ET... with margin for FRED\'s own 15-60min typical ingestion lag," which this exact incident disproved). Updated the one existing test that explicitly guarded the OLD narrow window (test_release_day_poll_is_armed_only_during_the_830_to_1000_et_window, which existed specifically to catch a future accidental widening "to run all day") — renamed and rewritten to assert the new, still-bounded hour="8-12" window plus the day_of_week="mon-fri" restriction, rather than deleted, since the underlying "must stay bounded, not unbounded" guard is still exactly the right thing to test. Adversarially verified: reverted the cron trigger back to the old hour="8-9" value and confirmed the updated test correctly failed before restoring the fix. Full 256-test event-intelligence suite green; pyflakes clean (confirmed via git stash — the 3 warnings shown, uuid/datetime/timezone unused imports, are pre-existing and unrelated to this change).\n\nNot fixed / deliberately out of scope: the underlying root cause is that FRED\'s own mirror of BLS/BEA data has no documented, guaranteed maximum ingestion lag — this fix widens the safety margin (2.5 hours -> 4.5 hours) rather than eliminating the possibility of a release posting even later than that. A release that FRED takes until, say, 2pm ET to mirror would still be missed under the new window; a fully robust fix would need either a distinct, longer-running "catch-up" check later in the day (e.g. one more poll at end-of-day) or a direct BLS-side signal instead of relying on FRED\'s own mirror timing — not attempted here, since today\'s specific gap (a release posting between 10am and 1pm ET) is now closed and a further-future occurrence would need its own investigation into how late FRED can realistically lag before deciding whether an even wider or entirely different detection mechanism is warranted.',
