    fix: '(1) Rewrite the reconciliation branch\'s message to lead with "Order placed successfully at the broker, but our own post-fill bookkeeping failed to reconcile: ..." — self-describing regardless of how any current or future renderer displays it — and remove the frontend\'s own "Real order failed:" prefix entirely, rendering broker_error verbatim instead of editorializing on top of it. (2) Add _sanitize_broker_error(), redacting long (24+ char) opaque alphanumeric runs (the shape of a bearer/session/API token) before truncating to 512 chars, applied at both raw str(exc) call sites and the reconciliation message.',
    implementedNote: 'FIXED 2026-08-13, same day as the fix it reviews. Found via a code-review pass (0fe376a...HEAD) run specifically because that range had not yet been reviewed — both findings were independently re-verified against the actual current code before being trusted: traced _place_broker_exit()\'s full reconciliation branch directly (confirmed the order-placed-successfully log line genuinely precedes the reconciliation catch, and confirmed no other consumer already distinguished this case) and traced EtradeBroker\'s own raise sites (confirmed every RuntimeError embeds resp.text verbatim) plus paper_portfolio.py\'s route decorators (confirmed get_current_user, not get_admin_user, unlike broker.py). A third candidate (whether portfolios are user-scoped, so only the "right" user could see this) was investigated and found to be a separate, pre-existing, out-of-scope design choice — this app\'s paper portfolios are shared/global across all logged-in users by design, not per-user-owned, so the real question was only ever "should ANY authenticated user see raw broker exception text," not an access-control bug.\n\nBackend: _place_broker_exit()\'s reconciliation branch now writes a self-describing message via the new _sanitize_broker_error() helper; both genuine order-failure sites (_place_broker_entry\'s outer catch, _place_broker_exit\'s outer catch) also route through it now, so token-redaction applies uniformly regardless of which branch is the real culprit. Frontend: BrokerStatusBadge no longer prefixes "Real order failed:" — it renders `error` verbatim (falling back to the pre-existing generic static title only when no error string is present), correctly covering the "synced" status too (a reconciliation failure sets broker_error on a trade whose _broker_status() still correctly resolves to "synced", since broker_order_id is present and the order genuinely succeeded — the earlier fix\'s status===\'failed\' gate on showing the real message would have silently hidden this exact case).\n\nA real pre-existing test needed a proportionate touch during this fix, not a rewrite: test_broker_fill_reconciliation_logging.py\'s own test_reconciliation_failure_records_broker_error_on_the_trade checks a fixed 400-character window after the log.error() call for "trade.broker_error" — my first version of the new self-describing-message comment pushed the real assignment line just outside that window, failing the test for a real but unintended reason (a verbose new comment, not a logic regression). Trimmed the comment to a single line rather than widening the test\'s own window, since the shorter comment loses nothing (the fuller reasoning already lives in the pre-existing AUD232 comment directly above it).\n\nTests: new services/market-data/tests/test_broker_error_sanitization.py (7 cases) — _sanitize_broker_error()\'s own behavior (ordinary short messages pass through unchanged, a long opaque token is redacted, truncation to 512 chars still applies after redaction, short ordinary words/codes are NOT swept up by the redaction pattern) via a real behavioral exec()-extraction of the pure helper function (no DB/broker dependency, so no source-text-only fallback needed for this half); source-text regression checks confirming the reconciliation branch\'s message text never implies the order failed and is routed through the sanitizer, and that both genuine raw-exception sites are too.\n\nAdversarially verified 2 sabotage cycles on the real file, both caught and reverted: (1) reverted the reconciliation message to its original "exit fill reconciliation failed: {exc}" form — 2 of 7 new tests failed correctly; (2) bypassed the sanitizer at both raw-exception sites (str(exc)[:512] instead of _sanitize_broker_error(str(exc))) — the dedicated cross-site test failed correctly, quoting the real unsabotaged source it expected in its assertion diff. Both reverts confirmed byte-identical (md5) to the pre-sabotage file before moving on. Full 1,286-test market-data suite green (up from 1,279); pyflakes clean (confirmed via git stash that all 3 pre-existing warnings predate this change, only shifted by the added line count). tsc --noEmit clean on the frontend change.',
