    what: 'Scoped explicitly before writing any code: both squeeze alert types depend on live, ephemeral state that has no historical equivalent — check_short_squeeze_alerts() reads stockai:live_prices (a 1-day Redis cache, no history kept), and check_gamma_unwind_alerts() makes a LIVE yfinance option-chain fetch (t.option_chain(exp)) for CURRENT open interest — yfinance has no historical options-OI API at all, and this app stores none either. A true "replay the live scan against 6 months ago" backtest is therefore impossible for gamma_unwind, full stop — building one would mean fabricating historical OI data, which this app\'s own standing discipline (see the CAPE/congress-data "verify against real data, never guess" precedent) explicitly refuses to do. short_squeeze IS partially reconstructable: its short-interest-of-float filter has real weekly history via FundamentalsSnapshot.short_percent_of_float (populated every Sunday since T230), and its intraday-move filter can be honestly approximated by a full trading day\'s own return (a conservative, non-inflating proxy for "a real move already in progress," since no historical intraday tick data exists either).',
    fix: 'New GET /admin/squeeze-alert-backtest endpoint. For every weekly FundamentalsSnapshot row clearing the real, live _SQUEEZE_MIN_SHORT_FLOAT threshold (imported lazily from scheduler.py, never a hand-copied duplicate value), scans that stock\'s daily Price bars for the trading days between THIS Sunday\'s snapshot and the NEXT one (point-in-time correct — a later, higher short-interest reading can never leak backward onto an earlier week that hadn\'t cleared the floor yet), flagging any day whose close-over-prior-close return clears the real, live _SQUEEZE_MIN_INTRADAY_MOVE_PCT threshold as a candidate day. Forward returns for each candidate are then scored using the EXACT same _squeeze_outcome_lookup_price() helper and _SQUEEZE_OUTCOME_WIN_HURDLE_PCT/_SQUEEZE_OUTCOME_WINDOWS constants the live daily evaluator (T264-SQUEEZEALERT-PERFORMANCE) already uses, so this can never silently drift into a second, differently-tuned scoring implementation. Below a configurable min_samples floor, reports an explicit "not enough data yet" note rather than a fabricated win rate. New Backtest section on the squeeze-alert-performance page (weeks-back selector, 5d/10d/20d win-rate/return cells), explicitly stating gamma_unwind isn\'t covered and why.',
    implementedNote: 'FIXED 2026-08-14. Tests: 11 new cases appended to services/market-data/tests/test_squeeze_alert_outcomes.py (now 33 total) — extracts the real squeeze_alert_backtest() source (stripping only its own local imports, which the test injects via namespace instead of re-running as real import statements) and runs it against the same real in-memory SQLite session as the rest of the file. Covers: finding exactly the one real qualifying candidate day (not every day in a snapshot\'s window), excluding a below-floor snapshot, excluding a below-threshold move, reporting a real win rate/avg return once forward windows resolve, reporting the below-sample-floor note instead of a fabricated rate, the point-in-time boundary property (two CONSECUTIVE qualifying weeks each contribute exactly one real move, never double-counted or dropped), and that the endpoint imports (not hardcodes) the real live threshold/scoring constants from scheduler.py.\n\nA real fixture-spacing trap was hit and self-caught while writing these tests: an early draft used price bars 5 CALENDAR days apart (not consecutive trading days), which produced a spurious second "candidate day" purely from the day-over-day comparison spanning more than one real trading day — traced by manually re-running the exact loop logic against both the sparse and a realistic densely-spaced fixture and confirming only the realistic one produced the intended single match; fixed by using tightly-spaced weekday bars in every fixture from then on (see _make_daily_bars()\'s own docstring for the full account).\n\nAdversarially verified 3 sabotage/revert cycles, all caught: (1) replacing the point-in-time window boundary with a fixed "always extend to today" window — initially did NOT fail the FIRST point-in-time test written, since that test\'s own fixture only ever had ONE qualifying snapshot in the loop (the below-floor snapshot never entered the per-symbol snapshot list at all, so there was no second window boundary to test against); investigated why the sabotage still passed, found the test\'s premise was wrong, and replaced it with a proper two-CONSECUTIVE-qualifying-snapshots fixture, which then correctly caught the sabotage (3 candidate days found instead of the correct 2 — the later window\'s move was double-counted under both snapshots\' iterations); (2) disabling the intraday-move threshold entirely — caught by 4 dedicated tests; (3) disabling the min-sample-floor guard — caught by 7 tests, including a genuine ZeroDivisionError the guard was silently also protecting against (a real correctness bug, not just a cosmetic message), confirming the guard is load-bearing, not decorative. All 3 sabotages reverted and confirmed byte-identical via md5 before moving on. Full 1,352-test market-data suite green; pyflakes clean (confirmed via git stash that both pre-existing warnings predate this change).',
